<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pakuan Pajajaran</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pakuan Pajajaran was the capital of the Sunda Kingdom in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, on the site of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogor">Bogor</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-baduga-maharaja">Sri Baduga Maharaja</a> ruled from it after uniting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-strait">Sunda</a> and Galuh in 1482 and strengthened it with a moat, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batutulis-inscription">Batutulis inscription</a> records. The Portuguese visited the kingdom in the 1520s. The Banten Sultanate captured and destroyed the city in 1579, ending the kingdom.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pakuan-pajajaran.jpg" alt="Pakuan Pajajaran" width="320" height="290" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hadiyana · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pakuanpajajaran.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The city, also called Dayeuh Pakuan, dayeuh being the Sundanese word for a capital, lay near the site of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batutulis-inscription">Batutulis inscription</a>, where the Cisadane and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ciliwung-river">Ciliwung</a> rivers run parallel for a few kilometres. Scholars have read its name in several ways: from rows of the king's fern or cycas, from paku, a nail standing for the king as the centre of the realm, from an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-javanese">Old Javanese</a> word for a palace, or, in H. Ten Dam's view, from its place between the two parallel rivers. The river valleys around it had been settled since at least the 5th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-baduga-maharaja">Sri Baduga Maharaja</a>, remembered in Sundanese tradition as King Siliwangi, moved the court here from Kawali in Galuh. Besides the wall and moat he raised sacred mounds and pavilions, set aside the Samida forest and dammed a lake, Sanghyang Talaga Rena Mahawijaya, probably for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> farming. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carita-parahyangan">Carita Parahyangan</a> remembers his reign as a just golden age, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bujangga-manik">Bujangga Manik</a> manuscript of about 1500 describes the court through the eyes of a prince who was also a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> hermit. The Portuguese envoy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tome-pires">Tomé Pires</a> described a great city of about 50,000 people, with houses of wood and palm thatch and a royal hall standing on three hundred and thirty wooden pillars.</p><p>After the loss of its port, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-kelapa">Sunda Kelapa</a>, the kingdom lived on behind the city's defences. In the 1550s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maulana-hasanuddin">Sultan Hasanuddin of Banten</a> sent his son Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maulana-yusuf">Maulana Yusuf</a> against it, and King Nilakendra could seldom stay in the capital. His successor Raja Mulya probably moved the court to Pulasari. In 1579 an official guard betrayed the gates, and the throne stone, watu gigilang, was carried off to the royal square of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banten">Banten</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>By the 17th century the city had been reclaimed by forest. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> expedition under Pieter Scipio van Oostende reached the ruins in 1687, reporting them guarded by tigers, and on 25 June 1690 Captain Adolf Winkler found a stone-paved road and a row of seven banyan trees. The buildings were probably of wood and thatch and have vanished; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batutulis-inscription">Batutulis inscription</a> still stands in place and is visited by pilgrims, and the site is revered as the spiritual home of the Sundanese people.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pakuan Pajajaran” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom’s centre shifted between <b>Pakuan</b>, near modern Bogor, and Kawali in the Galuh lands, where inscriptions commemorate the long-reigning king Niskala Wastu Kancana in the fifteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sunda-kingdom">Sunda Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1579 his son Maulana Yusuf captured <b>Pakuan Pajajaran</b>, ending the Sunda Kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/state/banten-sultanate">Banten Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>When Banten destroyed <b>Pakuan Pajajaran</b> in 1579, Sunda nobles are said to have carried the royal crown, the Binokasih, to Sumedang.</blockquote><a href="/state/sumedang-larang">Sumedang Larang</a></li></ul></section>
